2B [Actual] fell to $0.831B in FY24 [Actual] and $0.865B in FY25 [Actual], a ~38% peak-to-trough decline.\n2. The company is asset-heavy (net PP&E $5.538B FY25 [Actual]) and capex-heavy (FY23 capex $1.862B [Actual]), typical of cyclical transportation.\n3. Contract/spot truckload rates, intermodal volumes, and fuel spreads drive earnings; the 2026 recovery is visible in Q2 FY26 revenue +19% YoY [SEC press release], confirming cyclicality.\n4. A pure DCF without cycle normalization would dangerously overstate the cyclical trough as a permanent state; a pure quality-compounder multiple would ignore the demonstrated earnings volatility.\n\nArchetype transition check:\n- Not yet a Quality Compounder: ROIC is ~14% [Estimate], but margin volatility and freight-cycle dependence remain high.\n- Not a pure stable incumbent: revenue fell 4 consecutive years FY22\u2013FY25, which is inconsistent with stability.\n- Conclusion: no transition in progress; Engine 2 is appropriate.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 1. MACRO CONTEXT\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nStep 1-1 Macro variables:\n- 10-Year Treasury: 4.74% [Actual, FRED 2026-08-21], above long-run average 4.25%.\n- Fed funds target: 3.50\u20133.75%, unchanged at July 2026 meeting [Estimate, J.P. Morgan Global Research summary].\n- Real rates: roughly +1.5\u20132.0% on the 10Y [Assumption; breakeven inflation not retrieved].\n- DXY: [Unconfirmed] \u2013 not in data pack and not retrieved; treated as neutral in scoring.\n- Fed stance: neutral/hold with inflation caution [Estimate].\n- Global liquidity: not individually verified [Unconfirmed].\n- Economic cycle: Late expansion / early reflation. Freight demand is recovering: U.S. rail intermodal set a record in July 2026 [Estimate, AAR data center].\n- Credit spreads: not retrieved [Unconfirmed]. HY spreads are assumed normal in a late-cycle environment [Assumption].\n\nStep 1-2 Regime probability distribution:\n- Goldilocks (low rates + growth): 15%\n- Reflation (higher rates + growth): 50%\n- Stagflation (higher rates + low growth): 20%\n- Recession (low rates + low growth): 15%\nSum = 100%.\n\nStep 1-3 Macro sensitivity (-3 to +3):\n- Interest-rate sensitivity: -2 (capex- and credit-heavy).\n- Dollar sensitivity: -1 (transcontinental/intermodal trade exposure).\n- Liquidity sensitivity: +1 (freight volumes follow inventory/credit).\n- Economic-cycle sensitivity: -3 (highly cyclical).\n- Commodity-price sensitivity: -1 (fuel is a cost, but fuel surcharge and rail conversion partially offset it).\n\nStep 1-4 Expected macro impact:\nWeighted by the regime distribution, macro is a slight headwind. Reflation is the largest probability (50%) and is mildly positive, but the 35% combined Stagflation/Recession probability is significantly negative. Net: Neutral-to-mild headwind. Conviction -0.5 steps.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 1.5. BASE RATE CHECK\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nStep 1.5-1 Base rates:\n- 5-year survival for a large asset-based North American trucking/intermodal incumbent: ~90\u201395% [Assumption based on industry history].\n- Probability of a freight-cycle trough recovering to prior peak earnings within 3 years: ~50\u201360% [Assumption; 2021\u201322 peak to 2025 trough, 2026 recovery underway].\n- Probability of a wide-moat transportation name earning >WACC over the next 5 years: ~70% [Assumption].\n- M&A/narrative base rates: not applicable \u2014 JBHT is not a pre-revenue or TAM-narrative story.\n\nStep 1.5-2 Deviation from base rate:\nThe market price embeds a near-certain return to peak earnings plus a premium multiple. That is a deviation from the base-rate reality that cyclical peaks are followed by cyclical contractions. Three company-specific justifications are not sufficient to justify pricing >27x FY27 consensus EPS:\n1. Intermodal volume set records in 2026 [Actual/estimate from Q2 press release] \u2014 genuine but cyclical.\n2. DCS retention ~96% [Actual, Q2 press release] supports earnings floor.\n3. Management execution is high quality (10/12 years positive FCF before growth capex) [Actual].\nEven so, base rates say the current margin/volume level is above mid-cycle, not below.\n\nStep 1.5-3 Base-rate shock check:\nNo violation of the 1.5x rule; the issue is not success probability but the price paid for cyclical earnings.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nStep 2-1 Business understanding:\nJBHT is the largest U.S. intermodal truckload carrier, moving containers on rail for linehaul and trucks for drayage/final mile. It also operates Dedicated Contract Services (dedicated fleets), Integrated Capacity Solutions (brokerage), and Final Mile. Understandability: Medium \u2014 the business model is easy to understand; the earnings cycle is harder to forecast.\n\nStep 2-2 Moat assessment (0-3 each):\n- Intangible assets/brand: 2 (strong reliability reputation, but service is partially commoditized).\n- Switching costs: 2 (DCS contracts, intermodal service integration).\n- Network effects: 1 (density advantages, not true platform network effects).\n- Cost advantage: 2 (rail vs truck economics, scale in drayage).\n- Economies of scale: 3 (large container/rail network).\n- Regulatory barriers: 2 (operating authority, hazmat/rail partner requirements).\n- Data moat: 1 (moderate \u2014 routing/dray optimization).\nTotal = 13 \u2192 Wide moat.\nMoat direction: Stable-to-widening in intermodal (Eastern network +16% loads [Actual]); stable in Dedicated; narrowing in truckload brokerage (gross margin fell to 12.5% vs 15.5% YoY [Actual, Q2 press release]).\n\nStep 2-3 Lynch classification:\nCyclical. Core checkpoints: long-term volume growth exists, but EPS collapses in recessions (FY25 EPS $6.12 [Arithmetic] vs FY22 EPS $9.21 [Arithmetic]). Cyclical investors should pay mid-cycle, not peak, multiples.\n\nStep 2-4 Financial strength scorecard:\n- Revenue CAGR (5-yr): +4.5% [Actual, pack].\n- TTM revenue: $12.701B [Actual]; FY26E consensus: $13.67B [Estimate, S&P Global].\n- Operating margin latest: 7.2% FY25 [Actual]; 12-yr median 8.3% [Actual].\n- ROE: 18.4% [Aggregator].\n- Adjusted FCF (FCF - SBC): ~$1.03B TTM [Estimate].\n- Net debt: $1.704B including operating leases [Estimate] vs $25.87B market cap \u2014 modest leverage.\n- Interest coverage: ~12x [Estimate, using TTM EBIT ~$0.96B vs interest ~$0.08B].\n- Net debt/EBITDA: ~1.3x [Estimate].\nJudgment: Good.\n\nStep 2-5 Capital allocation scorecard (0-3 each):\n- Buyback efficiency: 3 (net issuance -4.9% 1-yr [Actual]; shares fell from 118M to 98M over 12 years [Actual]).\n- M&A track record: 2 (mostly small tuck-ins; no large impairments).\n- Capex efficiency: 1 (incremental returns have been mediocre; FCF was negative in 2014 and 2023 after large capex [Actual/Arithmetic]).\n- Mix rationality: 2 (growth capex + buybacks, but capex spiked into the 2022 peak).\n- Guidance accuracy: 2 (Q2 2026 beat EPS by 8.5% [Estimate]; historical track record not fully verified).\nTotal = 10/15. Insider ownership bonus: +1 (20.43% [Aggregator]) \u2192 11/15.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 2.5. ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u