ss net debt (LT debt $0.767B + current portion $0.700B \u2212 cash $0.017B) = $1.45B\n- Equity value: $10.7B\n- Per share: $10.7B / 93.9149M = $114 [Estimate]\n\nSensitivity (IV per share):\n- WACC 9.5% / terminal margin 7.5%: $85\n- WACC 10.8% / terminal op margin 8.3%: $115\n- WACC 12.0% / terminal op margin 9.5%: $160\nRange: $85\u2013$160; central $115 [Estimate]\n\nStep 3-6. Reverse DCF\nCurrent price $275.45 implies either:\n- Normalized EPS of $8.00 capitalized at 29x (vs 16x historical), or\n- Perpetual 8% growth on normalized FCF at 10.8% discount \u2014 not supported by a freight cycle.\nJudgment: EXCESSIVE.\n\nSECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n------------------------------------------------------------\n- Competitive response: If JBHT executes intermodal share gains, CHRW and Knight-Swift will\n  compete on price in brokerage/truckload; rail partner BNSF may push for a larger revenue\n  split (existing dispute). Impact on margins: \u22121 to +1.\n- Reflexivity: A high stock price aids SBC-based retention and gives M&A currency; a low\n  price would not damage customer trust materially (low bankruptcy risk). Neutral.\n- Customer evolution: As capacity tightens, spot customers migrate to contract; attach rates\n  and LTV improve. Risk: late-cycle volume loss if economy weakens.\n\nSECTION 4. SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n------------------------------------------------------------\n| Scenario | Probability | Key assumptions | 2027\u201328 EPS | Price target | Expected contribution |\n|----------|------------|------------------|-------------|---------------|----------------------|\n| Bear (recession) | 25% | Revenue $11.5B, op margin 6%, P/E 12x | $4.50 | $54 | $13.50 |\n| Base (cycle recovery) | 50% | Revenue $14.2B, op margin 8.8%, P/E 16x | $8.00 | $128 | $64.00 |\n| Bull (structural intermodal growth) | 25% | Revenue $15.5B, op margin 9.5%, P/E 15x | $10.00 | $150 | $37.50 |\n\nExpected price = $115 [Estimate]\n50% CI: $95 \u2013 $135 | 80% CI: $70 \u2013 $170