nue and operating income are strongly tied to the freight cycle: FY22 operating income $1.332B [Actual] fell to $0.831B in FY24 [Actual] and $0.865B in FY25 [Actual], a ~38% peak-to-trough decline.\n2. The company is asset-heavy (net PP&E $5.538B FY25 [Actual]) and capex-heavy (FY23 capex $1.862B [Actual]), typical of cyclical transportation.\n3. Contract/spot truckload rates, intermodal volumes, and fuel spreads drive earnings; the 2026 recovery is visible in Q2 FY26 revenue +19% YoY [SEC press release], confirming cyclicality.\n4. A pure DCF without cycle normalization would dangerously overstate the cyclical trough as a permanent state; a pure quality-compounder multiple would ignore the demonstrated earnings volatility.\n\nArchetype transition check:\n- Not yet a Quality Compounder: ROIC is ~14% [Estimate], but margin volatility and freight-cycle dependence remain high.\n- Not a pure stable incumbent: revenue fell 4 consecutive years FY22\u2013FY25, which is inconsistent with stability.\n- Conclusion: no transition in progress; Engine 2 is appropriate.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 1. MACRO CONTEXT\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nStep 1-1 Macro variables:\n- 10-Year Treasury: 4.74% [Actual, FRED 2026-08-21], above long-run average 4.25%.\n- Fed funds target: 3.50\u20133.75%, unchanged at July 2026 meeting [Estimate, J.P. Morgan Global Research summary].\n- Real rates: roughly +1.5\u20132.0% on the 10Y [Assumption; breakeven inflation not retrieved].\n- DXY: [Unconfirmed] \u2013 not in data pack and not retrieved; treated as neutral in scoring.\n- Fed stance: neutral/hold with inflation caution [Estimate].\n- Global liquidity: not individually verified [Unconfirmed].\n- Economic cycle: Late expansion / early reflation. Freight demand is recovering: U.S. rail intermodal set a record in July 2026 [Estimate, AAR data center].\n- Credit spreads: not retrieved [Unconfirmed]. HY spreads are assumed normal in a late-cycle environment [Assumption].\n\nStep 1-2 Regime probability distribution:\n- Goldilocks (low rates + growth): 15%\n- Reflation (higher rates + growth): 50%\n- Stagflation (higher rates + low growth): 20%\n- Recession (low rates + low growth): 15%\nSum = 100%.\n\nStep 1-3 Macro sensitivity (-3 to +3):\n- Interest-rate sensitivity: -2 (capex- and credit-heavy).\n- Dollar sensitivity: -1 (transcontinental/intermodal trade exposure).\n- Liquidity sensitivity: +1 (freight volumes follow inventory/credit).\n- Economic-cycle sensitivity: -3 (highly cyclical).\n- Commodity-price sensitivity: -1 (fuel is a cost, but fuel surcharge and rail conversion partially offset it).\n\nStep 1-4 Expected macro impact:\nWeighted by the regime distribution, macro is a slight headwind. Reflation is the largest probability (50%) and is mildly positive, but the 35% combined Stagflation/Recession probability is significantly negative. Net: Neutral-to-mild headwind. Conviction -0.5 steps.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u