550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nStep 2-1 Business understanding:\nJBHT is the largest U.S. intermodal truckload carrier, moving containers on rail for linehaul and trucks for drayage/final mile. It also operates Dedicated Contract Services (dedicated fleets), Integrated Capacity Solutions (brokerage), and Final Mile. Understandability: Medium \u2014 the business model is easy to understand; the earnings cycle is harder to forecast.\n\nStep 2-2 Moat assessment (0-3 each):\n- Intangible assets/brand: 2 (strong reliability reputation, but service is partially commoditized).\n- Switching costs: 2 (DCS contracts, intermodal service integration).\n- Network effects: 1 (density advantages, not true platform network effects).\n- Cost advantage: 2 (rail vs truck economics, scale in drayage).\n- Economies of scale: 3 (large container/rail network).\n- Regulatory barriers: 2 (operating authority, hazmat/rail partner requirements).\n- Data moat: 1 (moderate \u2014 routing/dray optimization).\nTotal = 13 \u2192 Wide moat.\nMoat direction: Stable-to-widening in intermodal (Eastern network +16% loads [Actual]); stable in Dedicated; narrowing in truckload brokerage (gross margin fell to 12.5% vs 15.5% YoY [Actual, Q2 press release]).\n\nStep 2-3 Lynch classification:\nCyclical. Core checkpoints: long-term volume growth exists, but EPS collapses in recessions (FY25 EPS $6.12 [Arithmetic] vs FY22 EPS $9.21 [Arithmetic]). Cyclical investors should pay mid-cycle, not peak, multiples.\n\nStep 2-4 Financial strength scorecard:\n- Revenue CAGR (5-yr): +4.5% [Actual, pack].\n- TTM revenue: $12.701B [Actual]; FY26E consensus: $13.67B [Estimate, S&P Global].\n- Operating margin latest: 7.2% FY25 [Actual]; 12-yr median 8.3% [Actual].\n- ROE: 18.4% [Aggregator].\n- Adjusted FCF (FCF - SBC): ~$1.03B TTM [Estimate].\n- Net debt: $1.704B including operating leases [Estimate] vs $25.87B market cap \u2014 modest leverage.\n- Interest coverage: ~12x [Estimate, using TTM EBIT ~$0.96B vs interest ~$0.08B].\n- Net debt/EBITDA: ~1.3x [Estimate].\nJudgment: Good.\n\nStep 2-5 Capital allocation scorecard (0-3 each):\n- Buyback efficiency: 3 (net issuance -4.9% 1-yr [Actual]; shares fell from 118M to 98M over 12 years [Actual]).\n- M&A track record: 2 (mostly small tuck-ins; no large impairments).\n- Capex efficiency: 1 (incremental returns have been mediocre; FCF was negative in 2014 and 2023 after large capex [Actual/Arithmetic]).\n- Mix rationality: 2 (growth capex + buybacks, but capex spiked into the 2022 peak).\n- Guidance accuracy: 2 (Q2 2026 beat EPS by 8.5% [Estimate]; historical track record not fully verified).\nTotal = 10/15. Insider ownership bonus: +1 (20.43% [Aggregator]) \u2192 11/15.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 2.5. ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nStep 2.5-1 R&D capitalization: minimal; not material. No adjustment.\nStep 2.5-2 SBC treatment: FY25 SBC $0.072B [Actual] is deducted from FCF.\nTTM adjusted FCF = $1.100B [Actual] - ~$0.072B SBC [Estimate] \u2248 $1.03B [Estimate].\nStep 2.5-3 Operating leases: Add $0.254B [Actual] to total debt. Adjusted net debt = vendor total debt $1.467B [Aggregator] + leases $0.254B [Actual] - cash $0.017B [Actual] = $1.704B [Estimate]. Our debt components used: vendor total debt + operating leases. We did not sum the overlapping XBRL debt columns.\nStep 2.5-4 Brand/data assets: not applicable.\nStep 2.5-5 Adjusted ROIC:\nTTM NOPAT = ~$0.96B op income \u00d7 (1 - 24.5%) \u2248 $0.725B [Estimate].\nInvested capital = equity $3.565B [Actual] + net debt $1.704B [Estimate] \u2248 $5.269B.\nAdjusted ROIC \u2248 13.8% [Estimate]. Above WACC (~10.5%) by ~3.3pp.\nStep 2.5-6 Owner earnings:\nFY25 owner earnings = NI $598M [Actual] + D&A $715M [Actual] - maintenance capex (~$500M [Assumption]) - \u0394WC (~$250M [Estimate]) - SBC $72M [Actual] \u2248 $491M [Estimate]. Cyclically, owner earnings are modest and lumpy.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 3. VALUATION (ENGINE 2 + FCF CROSS-CHECK)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nStep 3-1 Normalized cycle inputs:\n- Normalized revenue: $13.8B [Assumption] \u2014 between FY25 $12.0B [Actual] and FY22 peak $14.8B [Actual], reflecting recovery plus structural intermodal growth.\n- Normalized operating margin: 8.5% [Assumption] \u2014 between 12-yr median 8.3% [Actual] and FY22 peak 9.0% [Arithmetic].\n- Normalized operating income: $1.173B [Arithmetic].\n- Net interest expense: $40M [Assumption].\n- Effective tax rate: 24.5% [Estimate, company FY26 guidance].\n- Normalized net income: ($1.173B - $0.040B) \u00d7 (1 - 0.245) = $0.855B [Arithmetic].\n- Diluted shares: 93.9M [Aggregator].\n\u2192 Normalized EPS = $9.11 [Arithmetic]. I round to $8.90 to reflect a slightly conservative normalized margin of 8.3%. [Assumption]\n\nStep 3-2 Normalized multiple:\n- Historical fair P/E range for asset-heavy cyclical transportation: 18\u201322 [Assumption].\n- Base-case normalized multiple: 21.5 [Assumption].\n\u2192 Base-cycle IV = $8.90 \u00d7 21.5 \u2248 $191 [Estimate].\n\nStep 3-3 Explicit FCF forecast cross-check (DCF):\nDiscount rate derivation:\n- Risk-free rate: 4.74% [Actual, FRED].\n- Equity beta: 1.297 [Aggregator, vendor-window unspecified].\n- Equity risk premium: 4.75% [Assumption].\n- Cost of equity = 4.74% + 1.297 \u00d7 4.75% \u2248 10.9% [Arithmetic].\n- After-tax cost of debt: ~4.5% [Assumption].\n- Weights: equity 93.8%, debt+leases 6.2% [Arithmetic].\n\u2192 WACC \u2248 10.5% [Estimate].\n\nExplicit forecast ($B):\n| FY | Revenue | OCF margin | OCF | Capex | SBC | Adjusted FCF | Source |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2026E | 13.67 | 12.5% | 1.71 | 0.65 | 0.075 | 0.98 | Rev: S&P Global consensus [Estimate]; OCF margin: historical 12\u201314% [Assumption]; capex: consensus-implied [Estimate]; SBC: trend [Assumption] |\n| 2027E | 14.90 | 13.0% | 1.94 | 0.90 | 0.080 | 0.96 | Rev: S&P Global consensus [Estimate]; others [Assumption] |\n| 2028E | 15.60 | 13.0% | 2.03 | 1.00 | 0.085 | 0.94 | Revenue [Assumption]; others [Assumption] |\n| 2029E | 16.20 | 12.8% | 2.07 | 1.05 | 0.090 | 0.93 | [Assumption] |\n| 2030E | 16.70 | 12.5% | 2.09 | 1.10 | 0.095 | 0.89 | [Assumption] |\n\nTerminal growth: 3.0% [Assumption \u2014 below nominal GDP].\nTerminal value = $0.89 \u00d7 1.03 / (0.105 - 0.03) = $12.2B [Arithmetic].\nPV of FCFs + PV of terminal value \u2248 $10.8B EV [Arithmetic].\nEquity value = $10.8B + $0.017B cash - $1.704B net debt including leases \u2248 $9.1B [Arithmetic].\nPer share \u2248 $97 [Arithmetic].\n\nThis conservative DCF is the lower bound. It assumes capex returns to a $0.9\u20131.1B range and no cyclical premium. A more constructive DCF \u2014 FCF growing to ~$1.45B by 2030, WACC 9.3%, terminal growth 3.2% \u2014 gives roughly $209 per share [Estimate]. The fair-value range across the two DCF variants is approximately $100\u2013$210.\n\nStep 3-4 Reverse DCF / expectations check:\nAt $275.45, the market cap is $25.9B and EV is $27.6B. Consensus FY27 EPS is $10.12 [Estimate, S&P Global], so the market pays >27x forward EPS. To justify ~$275 on a 21.5x multiple, normalized EPS would need to be ~$12.80, which is 40% above the prior peak EPS of $9.21 [Actual, FY22]. To justify it on the DCF, FCF would need to grow ~8\u201310% for a decade \u2014 far above the historical FCF path. Feasibility: unrealistic on historical evidence. Conclusion: the market is pricing a quality-compounder outcome that the cyclical evidence does not support.\n\nTornado sensitivity (top 3 variables):\n1. Normalized operating margin (range 7.0\u20139.5%) \u2192 IV range roughly $160\u2013$215.\n2. Normalized multiple (range 18\u201325x) \u2192 IV range roughly $160\u2013$222.\n3. WACC (range 9.0\u201312.0%) \u2192 DCF range roughly $80\u2013$220.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n- Competitive response: If JBHT\u2019s intermodal model continues to take share, truckload competitors may cut rates; C.H. Robinson and Knight-Swift remain aggressive in brokerage [Estimate]. Effect: -2 on margin recovery.\n- Reflexivity: High stock price supports cheap SBC-based talent and acquisition currency; low stock price would be a minor negative. Net: +1.\n- Customer evolution: intermodal conversion from over-the-road is the mainstream adoption phase; volume records support this. However, margin per load will normalize as capacity returns. LTV/CAC not disclosed [Unconfirmed].\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 4. SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nPrior (from Section 1.5): cyclical freight earnings mean-revert; recovery has begun but peak multiplication is unlikely.\n\nScenarios:\n- Bear (25%): Freight recession resumes in 2027. Normalized EPS $6.50 [Assumption]; multiple 17x \u2192 Fair value \u2248 $110.\n- Base (50%): Recovery continues, operating margin normalizes to 8.5%. Normalized EPS $8.90 [Assumption]; multiple 21.5x \u2192 Fair value \u2248 $191.\n- Bull (25%): Intermodal growth and pricing persist; FY27 consensus EPS $10.12 [Estimate]; market pays 27x \u2192 Fair value \u2248 $273.\n\nExpected price = 0.25\u00d7$110 + 0.50\u00d7$191 + 0.25\u00d7$273 = $191 [Arithmetic].\n50% CI: roughly $160\u2013$220 [Estimate].\n80% CI: roughly $110\u2013$273 [Estimate].\nMargin of safety = ($191 - $275.45) / $191 = -44% [Arithmetic].\nJudgment: None.\n\nBayesian update applied: the Q2 FY26 beat (+19% revenue, +32% operating income [Actual/SEC]) increases the probability of the Base/Bull scenarios, but does not change the conclusion that the price already discounts bull-case earnings.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 4.5. TIME-HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2