s; increase entry discipline.\n\nSECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n------------------------------------------------------------\nStep 1.5-1. Base rates\n- 5-year survival for established asset-heavy logistics: >90% [Estimate, high confidence]\n- Probability of a cyclical freight company returning to prior peak margins after a recession: ~70% [Estimate]\n- Probability of 40%+ drawdown within 3 years from cyclical peak earnings: ~35% [Framework base rate]\n- Probability of a stock trading at >35x forward EPS at an early recovery being a value trap: ~50% [Estimate]\n\nStep 1.5-2. Deviation from base rate\nAnalysis recovery probability (Bull+Base = 75%) is close to base rate (~70%),\nnot >1.5x. No red flag. Company-specific justifications for recovery:\n1. Intermodal volumes +10% YoY (eastern network +16%) in Q2 2026 [Actual - company release]\n2. Dry-van contract rates +12% YoY June 2026 [Estimate - DAT/ACT Research]\n3. Company-wide operating income +32% YoY in Q2 2026 [Actual - company release]\n\nStep 1.5-3. Base-rate shock check\nSuccess probability (75%) < 1.5x base rate (~105%); no additional evidence required.\n\nSECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n------------------------------------------------------------\nStep 2-1. Business understanding\nJBHT operates the largest domestic intermodal container network in North America,\nmoving freight that combines truck and rail; it also runs dedicated contract\ncarriage, truckload, final-mile, and brokerage services. It earns revenue from\ntransportation services priced per load, with fuel surcharges passed through.\nUnderstandability: HIGH.\n\nStep 2-2. Moat assessment (0-3 each)\n- Network effects / density: 3\n- Switching costs (integrated customer relationships, service reliability): 2\n- Intangible assets (brand, technology): 2\n- Cost advantage (rail economies, container scale): 2\n- Economies of scale: 2\n- Regulatory barriers: 1\n- Data moat: 1\nMoat score: 13/21 \u2192 WIDE MOAT. Direction: WIDENING (road-to-rail structural shift,\nintermodal volume records, tight truckload capacity).\n\nStep 2-3. Lynch classification\nCYCLICAL. Not a fast-grower; earnings are driven by freight rates and capacity\nutilization. Checkpoints: revenue peaked at $14.8B in 2022, trough $12.0B in 2025;\nasset utilization and pricing are the key triggers.\n\nStep 2-4. Financial strength scorecard\n- Revenue CAGR (5-yr, FY2020\u2013FY2025): +4.5% [Arithmetic]\n- TTM revenue: $12.701B [Actual]\n- Operating margin (TTM): 7.2% vs 12-yr median 8.3% [Actual/Arithmetic]\n- Net margin (TTM): 5.3% [Arithmetic]\n- ROE (FY2025): 16.8% [Arithmetic]; vendor ROE: 18.4% [Aggregator]\n- ROIC (FY2025): ~13% [Estimate]\n- FCF (FY2025): OCF $1.678B \u2212 capex $0.731B \u2212 SBC $0.072B = $0.875B [Arithmetic]\n- FCF conversion: 146% of net income [Arithmetic]\n- Net debt: $1.467B (LT debt $0.767B + current portion $0.700B \u2212 cash $0.017B) [Arithmetic]\n- Net debt / EBITDA: $1.467B / ($0.865B + $0.715B) = 0.93x [Arithmetic]\n- Interest coverage (FY2024): 10.5x [Arithmetic]\nJudgment: GOOD \u2014 strong balance sheet, high cash conversion, but cyclical margins.\n\nStep 2-5. Capital allocation scorecard (0-3 each; total /15)\n- Buyback efficiency: 2 (reduced shares 34% since 2014; recent buybacks at $150\u2013$220 reasonable)\n- M&A track record: 1 (no major deals evidenced; [Unconfirmed])\n- Capex efficiency: 2 (heavy 2022\u20132023 capex now supporting intermodal growth; incremental ROIC likely > WACC)\n- Rationality of mix: 2 (balance of reinvestment and buybacks, no dividend excess)\n- Guidance accuracy: 2 (Q1/Q2 2026 beats of 27% and 8.5% vs consensus [Actual])\nInsider bonus: Management/family >5% ownership: +1. No recent insider buys/sells [Unconfirmed].\nCapital allocation score: 10/15.\n\nSECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n------------------------------------------------------------\nStep 2.5-1/2. R&D and SBC\n- R&D: immaterial (not reported separately) \u2014 no adjustment\n- SBC: $72M FY2025 [Actual]; already included in operating income\n- Adjusted FCF = OCF \u2212 capex \u2212 SBC = $1.678B \u2212 $0.731B \u2212 $0.072B = $0.875B [Arithmetic]\n\nStep 2.5-3. Operating leases\n- Operating leases: $0.254B FY2025 [Actual]. If capitalized and added to net debt,\n  net debt/EBITDA rises to (1.467+0.254)/(1.580) = 1.09x [Arithmetic]\n- Not included in primary net debt for consistency with vendor total-debt definition.\n\nStep 2.5-4. Brand/data assets\n- No separate brand or data asset value estimated; logistics value is operational.\n  Adjustment immaterial.\n\nStep 2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC\n- Adjusted NOPAT (FY2025): $0.865B \u00d7 (1\u221224%) = $0.657B [Estimate]\n- Adjusted invested capital = equity $3.565B + net debt $1.467B = $5.032B [Arithmetic]\n- Adjusted ROIC \u2248 13.1% [Estimate]\n- Gap to GAAP ROIC small; no material adjustment.\n\nStep 2.5-6. Owner earnings (Buffett)\n- Owner earnings (FY2025) = NI $0.598B + D&A $0.715B \u2212 maintenance capex [est. $0.550B]\n  \u2212 \u0394WC [\u2212$0.424B source] \u2248 $1.19B [Estimate]. Excluding WC release: ~$0.76B.\n- Conservative owner-earnings mid-cycle: $0.80\u2013$1.00B [Assumption]\n\nSECTION 3. VALUATION \u2014 ENGINE 2 (CYCLE-ADJUSTED)\n------------------------------------------------------------\nStep 3-1. Cycle position\n- Peak revenue: $14.814B (2022); trough revenue: $11.999B (2025)\n- Peak operating margin: 9.0% (2022); trough: 6.9% (2024); TTM: 7.2%\n- Recovery signals: Q2 2026 revenue +19.4% YoY, operating income +32% YoY,\n  intermodal volume +10% YoY, contract rates +12% YoY [Actual/Estimate]\n- Cycle position: EARLY/MID RECOVERY\n\nStep 3-2. Explicit 10-year normalized forecast (2026\u20132030) \nAll forward values are [Assumption] unless tagged otherwise.\n\n| Year | Revenue ($B) | Op Margin | EBIT ($B) | NOPAT ($B) | D&A ($B) | Capex ($B) | \u0394WC ($B) | FCFF ($B) |\n|------|--------------|-----------|-----------|------------|----------|------------|----------|-----------|\n| 2026 | 