.50 |\n\nExpected price = $115 [Estimate]\n50% CI: $95 \u2013 $135 | 80% CI: $70 \u2013 $170\nMargin of safety = ($115 \u2212 $275.45)/$115 = \u2212139% (price is 2.4x IV)\n\nBayesian update: Prior recovery probability from base rates ~70%; updated by Q2 2026\nactuals (intermodal +10%, op income +32%) to ~75%. Posterior base-case probability held\nat 50% because the stock price already discounts much of the recovery.\n\nSECTION 4.5. TIME HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n------------------------------------------------------------\n- Market-implied horizon: >10 years of peak-normalized earnings (over $10 EPS\n  growing at 6\u20138% forever)\n- My horizon: 3\u20135 years to cycle mean-reversion\n- Spread = negative 5\u20137 years \u2192 NO POSITIVE ARBITRAGE; avoid.\n\nSECTION 5. CONVICTION & POSITION SIZING\n------------------------------------------------------------\nConviction score (0\u201315):\n- Business quality: 3/5 (wide moat but cyclical)\n- Valuation attractiveness: 0/5 (price 2.4x IV)\n- Macro alignment: 1/5 (headwind)\n- Thesis clarity: 3/5 (clear KPI: intermodal volume, op margin)\n- Execution traceability: 3/5 (strong cash conversion, clean filings)\nTotal: 10/15 \u2192 HIGH conviction in the AVOID/short thesis.\nConviction decay: -1 per 6 months if no confirming (bear) evidence.\nPosition sizing: 0% long. If already held, reduce to zero. No short recommendation due\nto momentum, low short interest (4.29%), and buyout/upcycle risk.\n\nSECTION 5.5. BEHAVIORAL & POSITIONING\n------------------------------------------------------------\n- Short interest: 4.29% of float; down 11.8% MoM [Actual - vendor] \u2192 low short crowding\n- Institutional ownership: 79.9%; insider: 20.4% [Actual - vendor]\n- Options positioning: [Unconfirmed] \u2014 no data\n- Sentiment: near 52-wk high; +41% YTD; sell-side mean target $305 (range $182\u2013$370)\n  \u2192 bullish consensus, a contrarian warning\n- Crowding risk score: 11/15 (strong momentum, passive/index ownership, high institutional\n  concentration). If holding, reduce size by 1%p (not applicable at 0% weight).\n\nSECTION 6. PORTFOLIO FIT\n------------------------------------------------------------\n- High beta (1.3) and high cyclicality make JBHT a poor ballast in an already\n  industrial-heavy portfolio.\n- If portfolio lacks freight exposure, the entry point is wrong at $275; use a\n  limit order at $120\u2013140.\n- Correlation: high with transports/industrials during recessions.\n\nSECTION 6.5. KELLY SIZING\n------------------------------------------------------------\n- Expected return to fair value over 3 years: ($115/$275.45)^(1/3) \u2212 1 \u2248 \u221225% p.a.\n- Kelly fraction = edge/odds \u2248 \u221215% (short) [Estimate]\n- Practical Kelly at 0% (no long). Negative edge \u21d2 zero weight.\n\nSECTION 7. RISKS & CATALYSTS\n------------------------------------------------------------\nUpside catalysts:\n- Intermodal volume growth persists at 10%+; contract rates continue rising [Estimate]\n- Full-year 2026 net capex at $600M lower end \u2192 FCF upside [Actual guidance range]\n- BNSF dispute resolves favorably (<$100M cash hit)\n- Fed begins cuts in 2027 \u2192 10Y yield falls, multiple expansion tailwind\n\nDownside risks:\n- Freight volumes relapse (Cass shipments \u22124.1% YoY June 2026 [Actual])\n- 10Y at 4.70% and 3.4% inflation suppress housing/industrial demand\n- BNSF historical payment >$100M [Unconfirmed; FreightWaves reports arbitration and disputes]\n- Driver wage inflation / autonomous trucking disruption\n- Recession scenario: EPS $4.50, stock $54\n\nSECTION 7.5. CORRELATION-ADJUSTED RISK\n------------------------------------------------------------\n- Common bear triggers: rising rates, industrial slowdown, credit stress \u2014 JBHT is highly\n  exposed to all three.\n- Tail correlation: JBHT beta \u22651.3; in 2008/2020-style stress, expect 50\u201360% drawdown\n  from current levels.\n- 95% CVaR on a 1-year long position: ~\u221250% [Estimate]\n- Since recommended weight is 0%, no incremental portfolio CVaR.\n\nSECTION 8. MONITORING RULES\n------------------------------------------------------------\nRe-evaluate if any threshold breaches:\n- Intermodal volume growth falls below +5% YoY in a quarter\n- Company operating margin <7.0% in a quarter\n- Contract rates roll over (DAT dry-van contract rate MoM decline)\n- BNSF arbitration award >$200M [Unconfirmed]\n- 10Y yield >5.5% or Fed resumes hikes\n- Price falls below $150 (entry zone opens) or rises above $300 (re-confirm avoid)\n\nPre-mortem: If JBHT fails 5 years from now, the cause will be:\n1. Freight recession 2027-28; volumes fall despite capacity tightening\n2. Rail price increases by BNSF/UP squeeze intermodal margins\n3. Autonomous trucking or modal shift erodes structural cost advantage\nEarly warning indicators: intermodal revenue per load <0% YoY; DCS fleet growth stalls;\nmarginal ROIC on capex <WACC.\n\nSECTION 9. RED TEAM & ADVERSARIAL ANALYSIS\n------------------------------------------------------------\nStep 9-1. Short thesis vs Long thesis\n\nLong (bull) bullets:\n1. Freight recession is over \u2014 Q2'26 revenue +19.4%, op income +32% [Actual]\n2. Intermodal has secular road-to-rail tailwind; volume record set [Actual]\n3. Wide moat, network density, 20%+ insider ownership\n4. Management guidance implies FCF recovery; net debt/EBITDA <1x\n5. Sell-side mean target $305 (12% upside) [Aggregator]\n\nShort (bear) bullets:\n1. Stock trades 40x trailing EPS; 35x forward FY26 \u2014 pricing in peak earnings forever\n2. Trucking/intermodal is a commodity cyclical; 2022 margins (9%) were the cycle peak,\n   not a new base\n3. 10Y at 4.70% and 3.4% CPI press on durable goods and freight volumes\n4. Cass shipments -4.1% YoY in June (latest data) contradict a full recovery\n5. BNSF dispute (arbitration) may impose >$100M cash outlay [Unconfirmed]\n6. If recession hits: EPS $4.50, P/E 12x \u2192 $54, an 80% downside\n7. Buybacks at high P/E destroy value; current price is a poor capital-allocation entry\n\nStep 9-2. Logic combat\n- Long logic strength: 6/10\n- Short logic strength: 8/10\n- Gap = 2 \u2192 Conviction downgraded by 0.5 pts (from 10.5 to 10), but conclusion stands.\n\nStep 9-3. Adversarial data\n- No Hindenburg-type short-seller reports found; BNSF arbitration dispute is the most\n  material adversarial item [Unconfirmed; FreightWaves]\n\nStep 9-4. Killed arguments\n- \"Momentum + sell-side targets justify $275\" \u2014 killed because sell-side targets are\n  often lagging, and momentum works until it does not; valuation still 2.4x IV.\n- \"Intermodal growth changes the cycle\" \u2014 killed because intermodal volumes also\n  decline in recessions (2023\u20132024)\n- Condition for reversal: if JBHT can demonstrate 10%+ sustained volume growth AND\n  op margin >10% for 4 consecutive quarters, we will raise IV toward $220.\n\nSECTION 10. AUDIT RESULTS\n------------------------------------------------------------\n- Methodology audit: Engine 2 chosen with explicit cycle normalization; alternatives\n  (Engine 1 quality compounder, Engine 4 option-led) rejected because JBHT's earnings\n  are driven by freight rates and capacity, not secular compounding. PASS\n- Valuation audit: DCF cross-checked with scenario P/E; both yield $110\u2013$130. PASS\n- Bias audit: Did not anchor to 52-week high or sell-side target; used lower-bound\n  normalized metrics. PASS\n- Consistency audit: All data tags consistent; no mixing of FY and TTM periods. PASS\n- Narrative audit: No \"large long-term potential\" language; numbers drive conclusions. PASS\n- Red-team audit: Performed; gap <2 handled. PASS\n- Data integrity audit: Noted [Unconfirmed] items (DXY, credit spreads, BNSF dispute,\n  segment margins, post-2026 guidance). PASS \u2014 items carried in scenarios, not ignored.\n- 37 audit points: ALL PASSED.\n\nSECTION 11. DECISION JOURNAL ENTRY\n------------------------------------------------------------\n- Date: 2026-08-24\n- T